   "source": [
    "# Military Applications\n",
    "\n",
    "Every algorithm we have studied so far was introduced through **a problem we could run**: a frozen lake, a cart balancing a pole, an Atari screen. The environment was always available, always fast, and always exactly the thing we cared about. **Military decision-making** is an **attractive** target for RL for reasons that should by now sound familiar. The problems are sequential: a decision taken now constrains the decisions available later. They are stochastic: weapons miss, sensors fail, weather changes. They are partially observable: nobody sees the whole battlespace. They involve long horizons and delayed consequences, which is precisely the regime in which planning by hand becomes hard and learning from experience becomes attractive. And they are adversarial, which is the setting in which some of the most visible successes of modern RL, from chess to StarCraft, have been achieved. The same list, read again, explains why the domain is **difficult**. Sequentiality combined with long horizons produces sparse and delayed rewards. Partial observability means that observations generally do not satisfy the Markov property. Adversaries make the environment non-stationary. And, above all, the real environment cannot be used for trial and error: an agent cannot learn what happens if it strips air defence from a sector simply by trying it and observing the return. This limitation strikes at the core of reinforcement learning, whose defining features are precisely trial-and-error interaction and learning from delayed consequences. Here we examines one specific relationship: reinforcement learning and military simulation. The central question is not simply whether RL can solve military decision-making problems, but **what must a simulation provide for experience acquired inside it to produce policies that remain useful outside it?** The remarkable success of RL in games makes this question tempting to overlook; the gap between a game and a military operation makes it impossible to avoid.\n",
    "\n",
    "![image.png](attachment:image.png)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f297ba2e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Simulation-to-reality gap\n",
    "\n",
    "In every earlier chapter, we used a simulator largely for **convenience**: Gymnasium is faster, cheaper, and easier to control than a physical cart-pole. In the military domain, simulation plays a fundamentally different role. It is not merely a convenient substitute for the real environment; **it is what makes reinforcement learning possible at all**. Three independent obstacles prevent an agent from learning directly through real-world interaction, and any one of them would already be sufficient.\n",
    "\n",
    "The first is **safety**. An RL agent learns by taking actions before it fully understands their consequences. Exploration is not an accidental side effect of reinforcement learning; it is part of the learning mechanism itself. In a domain where a poor exploratory action can destroy equipment, compromise a mission, or kill people, this learning process cannot simply be transferred to the real world. The [RAND risk assessment](./papers/(2024)%20-%20Risk%20Assessment%20of%20Reinforcement%20Learning%20AI%20Systems.pdf)⁠￼emphasizes precisely this tension: RL requires extensive interaction, while learning may produce unsafe behaviour along the way. Simulation therefore provides a space in which the agent can explore, fail, and learn without imposing the corresponding real-world consequences.\n",
    "\n",
    "The second obstacle is **data volume**. Modern RL algorithms are extraordinarily data-hungry. A DQN agent learning an Atari game may consume tens of millions of frames; AlphaStar training accumulated, for individual agents, experience equivalent to as much as 200 years of real-time StarCraft play, as reported in the [wargaming study](./papers/(2025)%20-%20Deep%20Reinforcement%20Learning%20in%20Wargaming.pdf)⁠. No comparable supply of real military experience exists. At the level of operational or strategic decision-making, the problem is even more severe: the number of relevant decisions that have actually occurred and been recorded may be tiny compared with the number of interactions required by an RL algorithm. Even a perfect archive of past operations would therefore provide only a small fraction of the experience required for learning.\n",
    "\n",
    "The third obstacle is more fundamental: **counterfactuals**. Historical data tell us what decision was made and what happened next. They do not tell us what would have happened had a different decision been made. Yet RL requires exactly this kind of interaction. The agent must be free to choose actions (including unusual, suboptimal, and sometimes plainly bad ones) and then observe how the environment responds. A historical record can replay the past, but it cannot react to a decision that was never taken. A simulator can.\n",
    "\n",
    "Simulation is therefore not merely a convenience in this domain, it is the environment in which the RL learning loop becomes possible. But this solves one problem by creating another. If the agent learns entirely through simulated experience, then what it learns is initially **a policy for the simulated world**, not necessarily for the real one. Its apparent competence is therefore inseparable from the assumptions, abstractions, dynamics, adversary models, and reward structure encoded in that simulator. The central problem is no longer simply whether an RL algorithm can learn a good policy, but whether the simulated environment is **faithful in precisely those respects that matter for the policy** to remain useful when the world is no longer simulated.\n",
    "\n",
    "The final formulation sets up the natural next topic very well: the **simulation-to-reality gap**. It shifts the question from \"Can RL solve the simulator?\" to the much more important \"What does success inside the simulator actually tell us about success outside it?\""
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0a50d837",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Games are a good analogy, and a misleading one\n",
    "\n",
    "The modern interest in RL for defence applications comes directly from **games**. The chain of results is genuinely impressive, and the RAND report reproduces a useful comparison of the games that have been solved, ordered roughly by how hard they were for an AI system:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Game | Players | State space | Real time | Information | Stochasticity | Cooperation | Horizon |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Chess | 2 | $10^{47}$ | no | perfect | deterministic | no | ~80 |\n",
    "| Go | 2 | $10^{170}$ | no | perfect | deterministic | no | ~300 |\n",
    "| Poker | 2 or more | $10^{161}$ | no | imperfect | stochastic | no | varies |\n",
    "| StarCraft | 2 | $10^{1685}$ | yes | imperfect | stochastic | no | long |\n",
    "| Dota 2 | 10 | not estimated | yes | imperfect | stochastic | yes | long |\n",
    "\n",
    "Read as a progression, the table is an argument by extrapolation. Each row adds a feature that a military problem also has: more players, hidden information, randomness, real-time decisions, teams. [Go](./papers/(2017)%20-%20Mastering%20the%20game%20of%20Go%20without%20human%20knowledge.pdf) was mastered without human knowledge]by self-play alone, [StarCraft II](./papers/(2019)%20-%20Grandmaster%20level%20in%20StarCraft%20II%20using%20multi-agent%20reinforcement%20learning.pdf) was played at grandmaster level, [Dota 2](./papers/(2019)%20-%20Dota%202%20with%20Large%20Scale%20Deep%20Reinforcement%20Learning.pdf) was won by a team of five learned agents, [poker](./papers/(2017)%20-%20Superhuman%20AI%20for%20heads-up%20no-limit%20poker%20-%20Libratus%20beats%20top%20professionals.pdf) a game of imperfect information, fell to Libratus. If the trend continues, why not an air operations centre? The answer is that the progression measures the wrong axis. Every row of that table gets **combinatorially harder**, and none of them gets harder in the way that matters most."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### In a game, the game is reality\n",
    "\n",
    "When an agent plays chess against a chess program, the program is not a model of some external chess reality. For the purposes of the task, it is chess. The legal moves, state transitions, terminal conditions, and objective are completely specified by the rules. There is **no hidden physical process** that the program is attempting to approximate. If the rules are implemented correctly, there is **no gap** between the environment in which the agent learns and the environment in which its performance is evaluated. The same principle largely extends to Go, StarCraft II, and Dota 2. The environments become vastly more complicated, partially observable, stochastic, real-time, and multi-agent, but they **remain formally specified worlds**. Their complexity makes them difficult to solve, however it does not create uncertainty about whether the environment being solved is the environment of interest.\n",
    "\n",
    "A military simulator is fundamentally different. It is a model of a physical, social, and adversarial reality that **exists independently** of the simulator. The simulator must decide which parts of that reality to represent, how accurately to represent them, and which parts to leave out. Every such choice introduces **assumptions**, that can become sources of risk.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Bias** can enter through the construction of the scenario itself. A simulation is designed by people who necessarily bring assumptions about how forces behave, how decisions are made, and how an adversary is likely to respond. Assumptions used to model Blue behaviour can therefore leak into the modelling of Red behaviour, producing an opponent that unintentionally reflects our own doctrine or expectations. An RL agent may then become extremely effective at defeating our model of the adversary rather than the adversary itself.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Missing or incomplete information** creates a different problem. Features of reality that are difficult, expensive, or simply inconvenient to model are inevitably abstracted away. RAND gives an intuitive example: if roads in the simulator are always passable, then the agent never experiences weather, damage, congestion, civilian traffic, or other conditions that might make them unavailable. The agent may consequently learn perfectly sensible logistics decisions for a world in which roads never fail—and dangerously brittle ones for a world in which they do.\n",
    "\n",
    "There is also a less obvious danger in the opposite direction: **too much or irrelevant information**. Adding more detail does not automatically make a simulator more useful for learning. A sufficiently rich simulated environment may contain features that correlate with successful outcomes during training even though they have no stable causal relationship with the task. An RL agent is free to exploit those correlations. This is closely related to ⁠observational overfitting: a policy can become dependent on incidental properties of its training observations rather than on features that generalise across environments.\n",
    "\n",
    "The asymmetry is therefore fundamental. In a game, **simulation fidelity is essentially fixed by the definition of the task**: implementing the game correctly gives us the environment we intend to solve. In military RL, fidelity is itself part of the learning problem. We must ask not only whether the agent has learned the simulator, but also whether the simulator contains the right dynamics, uncertainties, information constraints, adversary behaviours, and causal relationships for what has been learned to remain valid outside it.\n",
    "\n",
    "This changes what success means. A high return in chess is evidence that the agent is good at chess. A high return in a military simulation is, strictly speaking, evidence only that the agent is good at that simulation. Establishing that this competence transfers to the real decision problem requires an additional argument—one that no improvement in the RL algorithm, by itself, can provide."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Transfer between a game and reality\n",
    "\n",
    "One might hope that a policy trained in a simulator is at least a good *starting point*, to be refined later against the real thing. This is the standard pre-training argument, and it is how sim-to-real transfer works in robotics.\n",
    "\n",
    "The RAND report is sceptical, and its argument has two steps. First, the empirical evidence on transfer even *between* games is mixed: on simple games pre-training helps, but on more complex games there is **negative transfer**, where a pre-trained agent performs worse than one trained from scratch because it must first unlearn strategies that generalise badly. Second, and decisively, the refinement phase in this domain would consist of *repeating thousands of actual engagements* while the agent learns. If the pre-trained policy cannot be relied upon to perform well from the first episode, the cost of the learning curve is not measured in wasted GPU-hours.\n",
    "\n",
    "The conclusion drawn there is careful, and worth quoting in substance rather than dismissing: this does not show that RL is unsuited to the domain. It shows that **wherever the simulation cannot capture the necessary elements of the operating environment, there must be some practical way for the system to keep learning without unacceptable risk** — and if there is no such way, that particular application is out of reach."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Intransitivity and strategic depth\n",
    "\n",
    "There is a second, subtler difference, which comes from game theory rather than from engineering.\n",
    "\n",
    "A game is **transitive** if strategy quality behaves like a ranking: if $A$ beats $B$ and $B$ beats $C$, then $A$ beats $C$. Rock-paper-scissors is the minimal counterexample. Intransitive structure is not a curiosity: [Czarnecki and co-authors](https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2020/hash/ca172e964907a97d5ebd876bfdd4adbd-Abstract.html) argue that real-world games have a characteristic \"spinning top\" geometry, with a large intransitive cycle among mediocre strategies narrowing towards a transitive core near the top, and that this is exactly why training strong agents requires maintaining a *population* of strategies rather than a single improving one. A learner that only ever trains against its current best opponent can cycle indefinitely without improving.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Strategic depth** is a related notion: how many moves a strategy takes to play out. Chess and Go have strategies of considerable depth, which is part of what makes them interesting to humans and hard for machines.\n",
    "\n",
    "The RAND report's observation is that an operational-level military problem contains **many strategic depths at once**. Some interactions resolve in seconds, others over weeks; objectives can change while the episode is still running; and intransitivities may exist between the tactics, rules of engagement, and objectives of the two sides. A single policy would have to hold all of that simultaneously, and would have to do so without any guarantee that the conflict it trained on resembles the one it is used in.\n",
    "\n",
    "We will come back to intransitivity with a runnable example in the section on multi-agent settings, because it is the cleanest place to see self-play fail."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Formulating a military decision problem\n",
    "\n",
    "With the caveats established, we can do what we have done in every chapter: write the problem as an MDP and see what each symbol means.\n",
    "\n",
    "Recall the objects from the chapter on the RL problem. An MDP is a set of states $\\mathcal{S}$, a set of actions $\\mathcal{A}$, dynamics $p(s',r \\mid s,a)$, and a discount factor $\\gamma$. The agent follows a policy $\\pi(a \\mid s)$ and maximises the expected return $\\mathbb{E}[G_t]$ with $G_t = \\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\gamma^k R_{t+k+1}$.\n",
    "\n",
    "Nothing in that definition mentions the domain. What changes from one application to another is only the **interpretation** of the symbols, and the interpretation is where all the design work lives."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### A worked example: penetrating a defended area\n",
    "\n",
    "The [wargaming study](./papers/(2025)%20-%20Deep%20Reinforcement%20Learning%20in%20Wargaming.pdf) by Del Rio and co-authors uses a scenario that is small enough to state completely and rich enough to be interesting. An attacking aircraft must reach a set of target coordinates. Between its starting point and the target sits a surface-to-air missile (SAM) battery which can destroy the aircraft if it enters the battery's radar cone. The aircraft cannot fire back and has no countermeasures. The optimal behaviour is obvious to a human: fly around the threatened region.\n",
    "\n",
    "Here is that scenario as an MDP, with each component named and interpreted:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Component | Formal object | In this scenario |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Agent | the decision maker | the attacking aircraft's route-selection logic |\n",
    "| State | $s_t \\in \\mathcal{S}$ | position and heading of the aircraft, position and status of the battery |\n",
    "| Observation | $o_t$ | a $100 \\times 100$ grid encoding the aircraft's position, plus the sine and cosine of its heading |\n",
    "| Action | $a_t \\in \\mathcal{A}$ | turn $10°$ left, turn $10°$ right, or continue straight |\n",
    "| Transition | $p(s' \\mid s,a)$ | the flight-dynamics model, advanced by 20 s of simulated time per decision |\n",
    "| Reward | $r$ | $+10$ on reaching the target, $-10$ on leaving the map or being shot down, $-0.01$ per step otherwise |\n",
    "| Episode | trajectory to termination | ends on arrival, destruction, or leaving the map |\n",
    "\n",
    "Several design decisions in that table are worth dwelling on, because each is a small lesson.\n",
    "\n",
    "The **action space was deliberately impoverished**. The authors state the principle explicitly: choose the minimal number of actions needed to solve the scenario and no more, because every additional action enlarges the branching factor. Altitude and speed are held constant and turn angles are discretised. This is the opposite of the instinct to give the agent every degree of freedom the real platform has.\n",
    "\n",
    "The **time step is an aggregation**. The underlying simulator advances in steps of roughly 0.1 to 1 second. Learning at that granularity would be hopeless, so twenty seconds of simulation are collapsed into one decision step. The choice of decision period is a modelling decision with real consequences: the same authors use a finer 15-second step in a scenario where the *timing* of missile launches matters.\n",
    "\n",
    "The **small negative reward per step** is not decoration. Without it the agent has no incentive to take an efficient path, and with $\\gamma$ close to 1 many wandering routes would score almost as well as the direct one."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### From MDP to POMDP\n",
    "\n",
    "The example above is nearly fully observable. Most interesting cases are not.\n",
    "\n",
    "A **Partially Observable Markov Decision Process** adds an observation space $\\mathcal{O}$ and an observation model $p(o \\mid s)$, or more generally $p(o' \\mid s', a)$. The agent no longer sees $s_t$; it sees $o_t$, and $o_t$ is in general *not* a Markov state, so a policy $\\pi(a\\mid o)$ has no reason to be adequate. The standard remedy, following [Kaelbling, Littman and Cassandra](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0004-3702(98)00023-X), is to act on a **belief state**\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\displaystyle b_t(s) = P(S_t = s \\mid o_1, a_1, \\dots, o_t)$\n",
    "\n",
    "which *is* Markov, at the price of living in a continuous simplex over $\\mathcal{S}$. In deep RL the belief is rarely computed explicitly; it is approximated by giving the network a memory, either a recurrent layer or a stack of recent observations. The wargaming study takes the second route in its more complex scenario, encoding a **trace of the last four positions** of each moving unit directly into the observation image, so that the network can infer headings and closure rates that a single frame would not reveal.\n",
    "\n",
    "The military vocabulary for partial observability is **fog of war**, and the same study draws a distinction that the RL literature often blurs:\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Imperfect information** is not knowing the *state*: where the enemy units are, what they are carrying.\n",
    "* **Incomplete information** is not knowing the *opponent's objectives*. A hostile move may serve its apparent aim, or it may be a feint covering an objective elsewhere.\n",
    "\n",
    "Incomplete information has no clean POMDP formulation, because the uncertainty is about the other player's reward function rather than about the state. The authors note that although this is central to real conflicts, it is rarely represented in wargames at all — an example of the simulator omitting something that matters.\n",
    "\n",
    "There is a further observation in that paper worth keeping, because it is counter-intuitive and correct: **reducing the observation space makes the problem harder, not easier**. Hiding information from a player converts a deterministic transition into an effectively stochastic one from that player's point of view, which increases the branching factor. Partial observability buys nothing computationally; it only costs."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Tactical and operational levels\n",
    "\n",
    "Military practice distinguishes levels of decision, and the distinction turns out to matter a great deal for whether RL is applicable at all. The papers supplied for this chapter divide cleanly along it.\n",
    "\n",
    "| | Tactical / control | Operational / planning |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Example | missile guidance, evading a launch, flight control | air tasking, force allocation, campaign-level course of action |\n",
    "| Decision period | milliseconds to seconds | hours to days |\n",
    "| Episode length | seconds to minutes | days |\n",
    "| State | low-dimensional, physical | heterogeneous, multi-domain, partly qualitative |\n",
    "| Reward | measurable and frequent | sparse, delayed, contested |\n",
    "| Simulator | physics; high fidelity achievable | model of organisations and intent; fidelity unclear |\n",
    "| Evaluation | miss distance, tracking error | judgement |\n",
    "\n",
    "At the tactical end, the situation is comparatively favourable, and the supplied papers show it. [He, Shin and Tsourdos](./papers/(2023)%20-%20Computational%20Missile%20Guidance-%20A%20Deep%20Reinforcement%20Learning%20Approach.pdf) formulate missile guidance as a fully observable MDP in which the state is the engagement geometry $s_t = (r, \\lambda, \\dot r, \\dot\\lambda)$ — relative range, line-of-sight angle, and their rates — and the action is the lateral acceleration command. [De Marco, D'Onza and Manfredi](./papers/(2021)%20-%20A%20deep%20reinforcement%20learning%20control%20approach%20for%20high-performance%20aircraft.pdf) train a DDPG controller to fly a six-degree-of-freedom fighter model through waypoint sequences, with an observation vector made of tracking errors, aerodynamic angles and control-surface positions. Both problems have a state that is genuinely low-dimensional, a transition function given by physics, and a reward that can be measured. Both are, in RL terms, control problems that happen to have a military context.\n",
    "\n",
    "At the operational end the situation reverses. The RAND study deliberately chose **operational-level command and control** as its focus, using the air operations centre and its **air tasking cycle** as the concrete case: a nominal 72-hour, six-phase process producing an air tasking order that covers a 24-hour period, so that at any moment the organisation is executing one day's order while planning the next two. That is the structure of the episode. Notice what it does to feedback: the assessment phase does not produce instantaneous results, and the report records the workshop participants' observation that it may take **24, 48 or 72 hours** to learn how well the last decision went. An RL agent operating at that level receives roughly one informative reward signal per day.\n",
    "\n",
    "The gap between the two ends of that table is the single best predictor of whether an RL project in this domain is likely to succeed."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Reward design, and how it goes wrong\n",
    "\n",
    "In a game the reward is given: win, lose, draw. In this domain the reward is **designed**, and the design is where most of the failures live. Two failure modes pull in opposite directions.\n",
    "\n",
    "If the reward is **sparse**, the agent may never encounter it. If it is **shaped**, the agent may learn to farm the shaping instead of achieving the goal. The supplied papers contain a clean instance of each."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Sparse reward: the missile guidance example\n",
    "\n",
    "The obvious reward for an interception problem is a bonus for hitting the target and a penalty otherwise. [He, Shin and Tsourdos](./papers/(2023)%20-%20Computational%20Missile%20Guidance-%20A%20Deep%20Reinforcement%20Learning%20Approach.pdf) tried exactly that and report the outcome bluntly: the simple reward function was **ineffective**, and the agent would never see a positive reward within a realistic number of episodes, because the probability of a successful interception from random initial guesses is extremely low.\n",
    "\n",
    "Their solution is instructive because it is not an RL trick, it is domain knowledge. Classical guidance theory tells us that driving the **zero-effort miss** to zero produces a perfect interception. The zero-effort miss $z$ is the closest approach the two vehicles would achieve if neither manoeuvred from now on, and it is computable at every instant from the engagement geometry. Shaping the reward on $z$ converts a signal that appears once per episode, almost never positive, into a signal available at every step. The final reward in that paper also carries terms penalising commanded acceleration (energy) and rewarding small relative range, so that the agent trades interception accuracy against energy consumption and interception time.\n",
    "\n",
    "The general lesson is the one already familiar from the exploration chapter, but sharpened: **when the reward is sparse, the fix usually comes from outside RL**. Somebody has to know something about the problem. In domains where no such quantity exists — and at the operational level, there is no zero-effort miss for a campaign — this route is closed.\n",
    "\n",
    "It is worth knowing that shaping can be done safely. [Ng, Harada and Russell](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~russell/papers/ml99-shaping.pdf) proved that a shaping term of the **potential-based** form\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\displaystyle F(s,a,s') = \\gamma\\,\\Phi(s') - \\Phi(s)$\n",
    "\n",
    "leaves the set of optimal policies unchanged for any function $\\Phi$ over states. Shaping terms that are not of this form — including most hand-designed ones, such as the ZEM-based reward above — carry no such guarantee and may change what the optimal policy is. That is not an argument against using them; it is an argument for testing whether the learned policy still does what you wanted."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Reward hacking: the waypoint example\n",
    "\n",
    "The opposite failure appears in the flight-control paper. [De Marco and co-authors](./papers/(2021)%20-%20A%20deep%20reinforcement%20learning%20control%20approach%20for%20high-performance%20aircraft.pdf) describe it precisely, in the context of training an agent to fly through a sequence of waypoints: for a poorly defined reward, *the agent might approach the target point in space and fly around the waypoint in order to accumulate as much reward as possible instead of passing through it and finishing the episode*. Reaching the goal ends the episode and therefore ends the income stream. Loitering near the goal is, under that reward, strictly better.\n",
    "\n",
    "Their remedy is a design constraint that is easy to state and easy to forget: they build the reward out of **penalty functions whose maximum is not positive**. Every step costs something, so there is no configuration in which prolonging the episode pays. They also report a second, purely practical finding: a hyperbolic penalty, nearly flat inside the acceptable range and nearly linear outside it, trained better than a logarithmic barrier — a reminder that the *shape* of the penalty, not only its sign, affects learning.\n",
    "\n",
    "This is the phenomenon that [Amodei and co-authors](https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.06565) call **reward hacking**: the agent optimises the reward it was given rather than the objective the designer had in mind. The RAND report's version of the concern in this domain is considerably less comfortable than a loitering aircraft. It gives the example of an agent rewarded for target kills producing friendly fire in a poorly designed system, and points out that missions with multiple simultaneous objectives — its example is transporting refugees while defeating an adversary — invite exactly the kind of unforeseen side effect that a single scalar reward cannot rule out.\n",
    "\n",
    "The structural difficulty is this. A commander has a clear picture of what mission success looks like, but that picture rests on assumptions so obvious that nobody states them. The reward function contains only what was stated."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### A small experiment\n",
    "\n",
    "The cleanest way to see reward misspecification is to build a problem small enough that we can compute the optimal policy exactly, and then vary only the reward.\n",
    "\n",
    "We use a $7 \\times 7$ grid as a stylised **route-planning** problem. An agent starts at the top-left cell and must reach a target at the bottom-right. A circular region in the middle of the grid is covered by a threat: entering it terminates the episode with a large penalty. There is also a cell marked as an *intelligence collection* point, which is genuinely useful but is not the mission.\n",
    "\n",
    "The environment is a plain MDP, and we solve it with **value iteration** from the dynamic-programming chapter, so that nothing in what follows depends on the vagaries of a learning algorithm. Any policy we obtain is exactly optimal for the reward we wrote down. That is the point: the failure we are about to see is not a training failure."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "\n",
    "N = 7                                   # grid side\n",
    "START, GOAL = (0, 0), (N - 1, N - 1)      # mission: get from START to GOAL\n",
    "INTEL = (0, N - 1)                        # a cell that is \"useful\" but not the mission\n",
    "THREAT_CENTRE, THREAT_RADIUS = (3, 3), 1.2 # circular threatened region\n",
    "\n",
    "ACTIONS = {0: (-1, 0), 1: (1, 0), 2: (0, -1), 3: (0, 1)}   # up, down, left, right\n",
    "ACTION_NAMES = \"^v<>\"\n",
    "\n",
    "def in_threat(cell):\n",
    "    \"\"\"True if the cell lies inside the threatened region.\"\"\"\n",
    "    dr = cell[0] - THREAT_CENTRE[0]\n",
    "    dc = cell[1] - THREAT_CENTRE[1]\n",
    "    return np.hypot(dr, dc) <= THREAT_RADIUS\n",
    "\n",
    "def step(cell, action):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Deterministic move; the grid boundary blocks movement.\"\"\"\n",
    "    dr, dc = ACTIONS[action]\n",
    "    nxt = (cell[0] + dr, cell[1] + dc)\n",
    "    if not (0 <= nxt[0] < N and 0 <= nxt[1] < N):\n",
    "        return cell                       # bumping into the boundary leaves us in place\n",
    "    return nxt\n",
    "\n",
    "def is_terminal(cell):\n",
    "    return cell == GOAL or in_threat(cell)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now two reward functions. The first is the mission as we mean it: reaching the target is worth a lot, being destroyed costs a lot, and every step costs a little so that short routes are preferred.\n",
    "\n",
    "The second adds what looks like a harmless piece of shaping. Intelligence collection is genuinely valuable, so we give the agent a small bonus each time it occupies the collection cell. Nobody wrote \"loiter\"; the designer wrote \"collecting intelligence is good\"."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def reward_mission(cell, next_cell):\n",
    "    \"\"\"What we actually want: reach the target, survive, do not waste time.\"\"\"\n",
    "    if in_threat(next_cell):\n",
    "        return -50.0\n",
    "    if next_cell == GOAL:\n",
    "        return +50.0\n",
    "    return -1.0\n",
    "\n",
    "def reward_with_intel(cell, next_cell):\n",
    "    \"\"\"The same, plus a bonus for sitting on the intelligence collection point.\"\"\"\n",
    "    r = reward_mission(cell, next_cell)\n",
    "    if next_cell == INTEL:\n",
    "        r += 3.0                          # \"collecting intelligence is valuable\"\n",
    "    return r"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def value_iteration(reward_fn, gamma=0.99, theta=1e-10):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Exact value iteration on the deterministic grid MDP (see the DP chapter).\"\"\"\n",
    "    V = np.zeros((N, N))\n",
    "    while True:\n",
    "        delta = 0.0\n",
    "        for r in range(N):\n",
    "            for c in range(N):\n",
    "                cell = (r, c)\n",
    "                if is_terminal(cell):\n",
    "                    continue              # terminal states have value 0 by definition\n",
    "                v_old = V[cell]\n",
    "                V[cell] = max(\n",
    "                    reward_fn(cell, step(cell, a)) + gamma * V[step(cell, a)]\n",
    "                    for a in ACTIONS\n",
    "                )\n",
    "                delta = max(delta, abs(v_old - V[cell]))\n",
    "        if delta < theta:\n",
    "            break\n",
    "    # greedy policy with respect to the converged value function\n",
    "    pi = {}\n",
    "    for r in range(N):\n",
    "        for c in range(N):\n",
    "            cell = (r, c)\n",
    "            if is_terminal(cell):\n",
    "                continue\n",
    "            pi[cell] = max(ACTIONS, key=lambda a:\n",
    "                           reward_fn(cell, step(cell, a)) + gamma * V[step(cell, a)])\n",
    "    return V, pi"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def rollout(pi, max_steps=60):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Follow a deterministic policy from START and report what happened.\"\"\"\n",
    "    cell, trajectory = START, [START]\n",
    "    for _ in range(max_steps):\n",
    "        if is_terminal(cell):\n",
    "            break\n",
    "        cell = step(cell, pi[cell])\n",
    "        trajectory.append(cell)\n",
    "    if cell == GOAL:\n",
    "        outcome = f\"reached the target in {len(trajectory) - 1} steps\"\n",
    "    elif in_threat(cell):\n",
    "        outcome = \"destroyed in the threatened region\"\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        outcome = f\"never terminated: still at {cell} after {max_steps} steps\"\n",
    "    return trajectory, outcome\n",
    "\n",
    "for name, fn in [(\"mission reward\", reward_mission),\n",
    "                 (\"mission + intel bonus\", reward_with_intel)]:\n",
    "    _, pi = value_iteration(fn)\n",
    "    trajectory, outcome = rollout(pi)\n",
    "    print(f\"{name:<24} -> {outcome}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The first policy does what we intended: it walks around the threatened region and reaches the target. The second never reaches the target at all — it parks on the intelligence cell and stays there. With $\\gamma = 0.99$, an unbounded stream of $+3$ bonuses is worth more than the single $+50$ that ends the episode, so the optimal policy under that reward is to stop working on the mission. Nothing has gone wrong with the algorithm; the algorithm found the maximum of the function we wrote.\n",
    "\n",
    "Three details are worth noticing, because they are the general lesson rather than the particulars.\n",
    "\n",
    "First, **the bug is invisible in the reward function**. Every individual term is defensible: reaching the target is good, being destroyed is bad, time costs something, intelligence is valuable. The defect is in the *arithmetic of the return*, not in any single number.\n",
    "\n",
    "Second, **the agent used a mechanism the designer never considered**. The intelligence cell sits in a corner, and the environment says that a move into the boundary leaves the agent where it is. So the agent commands a move it knows will fail, in order to remain on the rewarded cell and collect the bonus on *every* step rather than every other one. Nobody wrote that rule with this consequence in mind; it was there to keep the agent inside the grid. This is exactly the shape of a reward hack: the exploit lives in the interaction between two innocuous parts of the specification.\n",
    "\n",
    "Third, **the failure has a threshold, and nobody computed it**. Staying put earns $b - 1$ per step forever, worth $(b-1)/(1-\\gamma)$; the direct route from that cell earns the discounted $+50$ minus six step costs. Setting the two equal gives $b \\approx 1.43$ — so the policy flips from \"accomplish the mission\" to \"never accomplish the mission\" between $b = 1.4$ and $b = 1.5$, and our chosen $b = 3$ was well past the cliff without anyone noticing. In a grid of 49 states the threshold can be found in two lines of algebra. In a simulation with millions of states and a dozen reward terms, it cannot."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<!-- FIGURE: two 7x7 grids side by side. Left, \"mission reward\": the threatened circle shaded red, a green path from the top-left start curving around the threat to the bottom-right target. Right, \"mission + intel bonus\": the same grid, with the path going up to the top-right INTEL cell and a small self-loop arrow on that cell, and no path to the target at all. Caption: the same environment, the same algorithm, one extra reward term. -->"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Multi-agent and adversarial settings\n",
    "\n",
    "Everything so far assumed a single decision maker. Military problems rarely have one.\n",
    "\n",
    "The standard generalisation is the **Markov game**, or stochastic game, introduced into the RL literature by [Littman](https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-1-55860-335-6.50027-1). With $N$ agents, the transition depends on the **joint action** $\\boldsymbol{a} = (a^1, \\dots, a^N)$, and each agent $i$ receives its own reward:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\displaystyle p(s' \\mid s, a^1, \\dots, a^N), \\qquad r^i = R^i(s, a^1, \\dots, a^N)$\n",
    "\n",
    "Each agent still maximises its own expected return, but the return now depends on policies it does not control. Three regimes are conventionally distinguished, and all three occur in this domain:\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Cooperative**, where all agents share a reward, $R^i = R$ for all $i$. A flight of aircraft executing a single mission; a set of sensors tasked to maximise joint coverage.\n",
    "* **Competitive**, typically zero-sum with two sides, $R^1 = -R^2$. Attacker against defender.\n",
    "* **Mixed**, where teams cooperate internally and compete externally. This is the realistic case, and it is the hardest. Dota 2 sits here, which is precisely why it appears at the bottom of the games table.\n",
    "\n",
    "The [review by Wang and co-authors](./papers/(2023)%20-%20A%20Review%20of%20Deep%20Reinforcement%20Learning%20Methods%20and%20Military%20Application%20Research.pdf) identifies task assignment as a natural fit for the multi-agent formulation, while also noting the practical obstacles: slow convergence when tasks are coupled, and the fact that most published studies assume ideal communication, in which every agent has access to the full environment state simultaneously — an assumption their own review flags as unrealistic."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### The non-stationarity problem\n",
    "\n",
    "There is a specific reason multi-agent RL is not simply single-agent RL with more inputs, and the [wargaming study](./papers/(2025)%20-%20Deep%20Reinforcement%20Learning%20in%20Wargaming.pdf) states it in its conclusions as the central limitation of their work:\n",
    "\n",
    "> The central assumption empowering RL is that the environment's transition probabilities remain fixed during the training. [...] this assumption is usually not satisfied in multi-party scenarios.\n",
    "\n",
    "From agent $i$'s point of view, the effective transition function is\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\displaystyle p_i(s' \\mid s, a^i) = \\sum_{\\boldsymbol{a}^{-i}} p(s' \\mid s, a^i, \\boldsymbol{a}^{-i}) \\prod_{j \\neq i} \\pi^j(a^j \\mid s)$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $\\boldsymbol{a}^{-i}$ denotes the actions of everybody else. Every symbol on the right is fixed except the other agents' policies $\\pi^j$ — and those are *also learning*. The environment that agent $i$ is trying to model is changing underneath it, in a way correlated with its own improvement. This breaks the stationarity assumption on which the convergence proofs of Q-learning and the policy-gradient theorem rest; [Hernandez-Leal, Kartal and Taylor](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10458-019-09421-1) survey the field that has grown up around this problem, and [MADDPG](https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2017/hash/68a9750337a418a86fe06c1991a1d64c-Abstract.html) is a representative response: train a centralised critic that sees all agents' actions, but execute a decentralised actor that sees only its own observation.\n",
    "\n",
    "Non-stationarity is not confined to learning opponents. A real adversary adapts between engagements, which means a policy that was optimal last month may be exploitable this month — a distribution shift that no amount of training on the old distribution can anticipate."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Why self-play can stall: an experiment\n",
    "\n",
    "The combination of non-stationarity and intransitivity produces a failure that is easy to demonstrate and easy to miss.\n",
    "\n",
    "Consider a stylised **force-composition** game. Each side commits to one of three postures — armour, air, or infantry — and the payoff matrix is intransitive: armour beats infantry, infantry beats air, air beats armour. There is nothing exotic here; it is rock-paper-scissors wearing a uniform. We then run the most natural self-play scheme imaginable: at each round, each side switches to the **best response** to what the opponent played last round."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "\n",
    "POSTURES = [\"armour\", \"air\", \"infantry\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "# A[i, j] = payoff to the row player choosing i against the column player choosing j.\n",
    "# armour beats infantry, infantry beats air, air beats armour  (an intransitive cycle)\n",
    "A = np.array([[ 0, -1,  1],     # armour   vs armour / air / infantry\n",
    "              [ 1,  0, -1],     # air      vs ...\n",
    "              [-1,  1,  0]])    # infantry vs ...\n",
    "\n",
    "def best_response(opponent_choice):\n",
    "    \"\"\"The posture with the highest payoff against a known opponent posture.\"\"\"\n",
    "    return int(np.argmax(A[:, opponent_choice]))\n",
    "\n",
    "blue, red = 0, 2                 # blue opens with armour, red with infantry\n",
    "history = []\n",
    "for round_index in range(9):\n",
    "    history.append((POSTURES[blue], POSTURES[red]))\n",
    "    blue, red = best_response(red), best_response(blue)   # simultaneous adaptation\n",
    "\n",
    "for k, (b, r) in enumerate(history):\n",
    "    print(f\"round {k}:  blue={b:<9} red={r}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The pair of policies chases its own tail: the joint configuration returns to where it started every six rounds and never settles. Neither side is learning badly — each is playing an exact best response — and yet the process converges to nothing. If we had stopped training at any round and declared the current Blue policy \"trained\", we would have shipped a policy that the very next Red adaptation defeats.\n",
    "\n",
    "Two standard escapes exist, and both are visible in the game-playing literature. The first is to **randomise**: the unique Nash equilibrium of this game is the uniform mixture over the three postures, which guarantees an expected payoff of zero against any opponent. The second is to train against a **population** of past opponents rather than only the current one, which is the league mechanism used in AlphaStar and the practical reading of Czarnecki and co-authors' spinning-top argument.\n",
    "\n",
    "The military reading of this small experiment is worth stating. A policy that is optimal against a specific modelled adversary is, by construction, **overfitted to that adversary**. The wargaming study makes this explicit: for complex wargames it considers a general game-playing agent unrealistic and recommends training on **pre-set scenarios**, accepting deliberate overfitting to scenario and adversary style, and it notes that agents so trained are limited by the versatility of the courses of action they have seen, which more versatile agents can exploit easily. Deliberate overfitting is a defensible engineering choice — but only if everyone downstream knows that is what was done."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Adversaries that attack the policy itself\n",
    "\n",
    "There is a second, sharper sense in which these settings are adversarial. Deep networks are vulnerable to inputs crafted to make them fail, and policies built on deep networks inherit the vulnerability.\n",
    "\n",
    "The RAND report describes a striking demonstration, from the work of [Gleave and co-authors on adversarial policies](https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.10615). Two RL agents play a physical game in which one must get past the other. Instead of blocking, the adversarial agent falls to the ground and flails. The victim agent, which has never seen such behaviour, collapses — rather than simply walking past, as any human would. The attack is not on the observations through a crafted perturbation; it is a *policy* that drives the victim into states its training never covered.\n",
    "\n",
    "The defensive analogue is **adversarial training**: [robust adversarial RL](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v70/pinto17a.html) trains a protagonist alongside a destabilising adversary, producing policies that tolerate model mismatch and disturbance better. But the RAND report's caution is the operative one: it is not clear that all such weaknesses can be found, especially in a broad application where thorough computational exploration is infeasible. A shared weakness across several fielded RL systems would be exploitable simultaneously, and taking those systems offline to retrain them is itself a loss of capability."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## The simulator is not the world\n",
    "\n",
    "We now return to the theme of the chapter with the tools to make it precise.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Fidelity costs speed, and speed is what learning consumes\n",
    "\n",
    "There is a trade-off in simulator design that has no counterpart in the game domain, and the [wargaming study](./papers/(2025)%20-%20Deep%20Reinforcement%20Learning%20in%20Wargaming.pdf) quantifies it. The authors used *Command: Modern Operations* (CMO), a professional-grade simulation with realistic physical dynamics and sensor modelling, and measured what it costs an RL algorithm:\n",
    "\n",
    "| | CMO | `war-sim` (their lightweight replica) |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Asset types | more than 100 | 2 |\n",
    "| Physical dynamics | accurate | approximated |\n",
    "| Simulation step time | ~1.5 s | ~0.1 s |\n",
    "| State restore time | ~0.1 s | ~0.001 s |\n",
    "| Engine startup time | ~15 s | ~0.1 s |\n",
    "\n",
    "*From the [wargaming study](./papers/(2025)%20-%20Deep%20Reinforcement%20Learning%20in%20Wargaming.pdf), Table 1.*\n",
    "\n",
    "A step time of around one second sounds harmless until it is multiplied by the number of steps RL needs. The authors make the multiplication for familiar benchmarks: at one second per action, training a tabular $Q$-learning agent on a $6 \\times 9$ grid world would take about **10 minutes**, and the mountain-car benchmark about **1.5 hours** — and that assumes the hyper-parameters are already right, with no tuning runs.\n",
    "\n",
    "Let us do the same arithmetic for ourselves, because it is the calculation that decides whether a project is feasible before any code is written."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def wall_clock(steps, seconds_per_step, workers=1):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Wall-clock time needed to collect a given number of environment steps.\"\"\"\n",
    "    return steps * seconds_per_step / workers          # in seconds\n",
    "\n",
    "def humanise(seconds):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Print a duration in whichever unit makes it readable.\"\"\"\n",
    "    for unit, size, limit in ((\"s\", 1, 120), (\"min\", 60, 120), (\"h\", 3600, 72),\n",
    "                              (\"d\", 86400, 400), (\"y\", 31_536_000, None)):\n",
    "        if limit is None or seconds < size * limit:\n",
    "            return f\"{seconds / size:.1f} {unit}\"\n",
    "\n",
    "# Interaction budgets of the kind used in the earlier chapters of these notes\n",
    "BUDGETS = {\n",
    "    \"tabular agent, small grid world\":   2e4,\n",
    "    \"mountain car (classic control)\":    5e5,\n",
    "    \"a modest deep RL run\":              1e7,\n",
    "    \"an Atari-scale DQN run\":            5e7,\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "LATENCIES = [(\"in-process Python env\", 0.001),\n",
    "             (\"lightweight simulator\", 0.1),\n",
    "             (\"high-fidelity simulator\", 1.5)]\n",
    "\n",
    "header = f\"{'interaction budget':<34}\" + \"\".join(f\"{name:>24}\" for name, _ in LATENCIES)\n",
    "print(header)\n",
    "print(\"-\" * len(header))\n",
    "for label, steps in BUDGETS.items():\n",
    "    row = \"\".join(f\"{humanise(wall_clock(steps, s)):>24}\" for _, s in LATENCIES)\n",
    "    print(f\"{label:<34}{row}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The table makes the point without much further commentary. The column on the left is the regime every earlier chapter of these notes lived in. The column on the right is the regime a high-fidelity military simulator lives in, and moving between them does not change the algorithm at all — it changes which problems are reachable. Parallelism helps but does not rescue the situation: the wargaming study's hardware could host up to forty concurrent CMO instances, which divides the right-hand column by forty and leaves an Atari-scale budget still measured in weeks.\n",
    "\n",
    "The empirical part of the wargaming study confirms that this is not merely an inconvenience. The authors ran PPO, DQN and an AlphaZero-style planner on the same scenarios in both environments. In `war-sim`, AlphaZero solved both the planning and the combat scenario and DQN solved the combat scenario. On CMO, with the *same* scenarios and the same hyper-parameters, **none of the algorithms converged**; AlphaZero managed only partial learning on the planning scenario, and its planner suffered from crashes during the save-and-restore operations that look-ahead search requires. The authors attribute the difference to environment meta-features — latency and stability — rather than to the scenarios themselves.\n",
    "\n",
    "This is the accuracy-versus-speed trade-off in its sharpest form, and their conclusion is the one to remember: **the best wargaming AI can be expected in intermediate environments that are reasonably accurate but still fast enough to learn in.** A perfect simulator that cannot be stepped a billion times is not useful to RL, and a fast simulator that is wrong is worse than useless.\n",
    "\n",
    "There is a related engineering point that the same paper raises and that is easy to underestimate: model-based methods need to *jump around* in the state space, loading states out of order to build a planning tree. Commercial simulators are built for forward play by a human, not for random access by a search algorithm. A simulator's suitability for RL depends on its API as much as on its physics."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Distribution shift and the limits of a trained policy\n",
    "\n",
    "A policy trained in one distribution of situations is guaranteed nothing outside it. This is **domain generalisation**, and the RAND report describes RL as especially prone to failure here because the shift has a temporal as well as a spatial component: a small difference in dynamics compounds over a trajectory.\n",
    "\n",
    "The missile guidance paper provides a compact, concrete instance. The agent was trained on the assumption that the target flies a constant course. When it is then tested against a target whose lateral acceleration is drawn at random, the interception still succeeds, but the commanded acceleration response **exhibits undesired oscillations** in some scenarios, which the authors flag as needing further investigation and tuning. Nothing catastrophic happened; the policy degraded in a way that was visible only because someone thought to test outside the training distribution.\n",
    "\n",
    "That is the benign version. The RAND report's version is that we do not, in general, know when an RL system will fail, nor can we predict how — which makes both *identifying* a failure that warrants retraining and *estimating how often* it will occur problematic.\n",
    "\n",
    "The standard countermeasure is to train on a deliberately widened distribution. In robotics this is **domain randomisation**: [Tobin and co-authors](https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS.2017.8202133) showed that randomising simulator parameters aggressively enough makes the real world look to the network like just another variation, allowing transfer with no real-world training at all; the broader sim-to-real literature is surveyed by [Zhao, Queralta and Westerlund](https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.13303). The RAND workshop participants arrived at the same idea from the operational side, and their phrasing is worth borrowing because it is so concrete: for an agent learning to route an aircraft around radars, include **a wider variety of radars in training than are expected to be encountered**, then focus on the expected ones for the remainder of training. They also record the caveat: participants judged this difficult and time-consuming for a narrow application, and possibly infeasible for a broad one.\n",
    "\n",
    "Domain randomisation buys robustness at a price in performance. Widening the training distribution means the policy is optimal for none of its members. In a domain where a small performance margin may matter, that trade is not automatic."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Learning without exploring\n",
    "\n",
    "If the agent cannot explore the real environment, and the simulator is imperfect, the remaining source of information is **data that already exists**. Two approaches use it.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Imitation learning** trains the agent to reproduce demonstrated behaviour. It is appropriate when many examples of expert interaction exist. **Offline reinforcement learning** goes further: it learns a policy from a fixed dataset of transitions, attempting to find a policy *better* than the one that generated the data rather than merely copying it. The RAND report summarises the distinction usefully — imitation is the right choice when the demonstrations are expert, offline RL performs better when they are suboptimal or noisy — and [Levine and co-authors](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.01643) give the standard technical treatment of the offline case.\n",
    "\n",
    "Both share a structural weakness, and the RAND report is explicit: they reduce the need for simulated data **only if the agent has access to many real-world examples**, and both remain vulnerable to distribution shift if the operational situation differs from the recorded one. For this domain that condition bites hardest exactly where the problem is most interesting. Logistics and repeated cargo movements generate plenty of examples. Operational-level command decisions do not.\n",
    "\n",
    "The Waymo experience reported in the same study makes a related point that transfers directly. Learning from *good* situations is easy because there are many of them: staying in lane, obeying traffic lights, turning. Learning from *bad* situations is hard because collisions are rare, so the dataset is biased towards the cases that were never the problem. Their response was to imitate the good situations and **synthesise** the bad ones by perturbing good ones. A military analogue is immediate: historical records are dominated by operations that went to plan.\n",
    "\n",
    "A third technique, standard in the game literature, is **supervised pre-training**: initialise the policy from human play, then improve it with RL. The wargaming study reports the magnitude of the effect for StarCraft — without pre-training, an AlphaStar-type agent reaches roughly 150 Elo, against roughly 900 Elo for a purely supervised agent — and then explains why they could not use it: no CMO gameplay data was available to them. The same paper reports that where expert data is unavailable, one can sometimes substitute a **curriculum on the reward**, beginning with an engineered immediate signal such as asset count and moving to the episodic mission-level reward once a given strength is reached. The missile guidance paper reaches an equivalent conclusion from the control side: leveraging prior knowledge during learning outperformed learning from scratch.\n",
    "\n",
    "The pattern across all of these is the same. **RL in this domain is rarely tabula rasa.** Something — a classical guidance law, a behaviour tree, a corpus of demonstrations, a shaped reward — has to carry the agent through the region where the reward signal is uninformative."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Scripted opponents, and what they teach\n",
    "\n",
    "A related question is what the agent trains *against*. The [BVR Gym](./papers/(2024)%20-%20BVR%20Gym-%20A%20Reinforcement%20Learning%20Environment%20for%20Beyond-Visual-Range%20Air%20Combat.pdf) environment by Scukins and co-authors answers it with **behaviour trees**: the adversary aircraft is controlled by a hand-designed, modular policy, and the learning agent's task is to find tactics that defeat that known behaviour.\n",
    "\n",
    "Their environment is a good illustration of the whole chapter in miniature, so it is worth reading as one. Beyond-visual-range air combat is fought at up to 100 km, which means the agent cannot observe the incoming missile directly — the missile is too small for radar at that range — so the observation includes only the *launch* location, inferred from a detected flash, together with the aircraft's own state. The engagement plays out slowly, which the authors identify as the reason the rewards are sparse and training is hard: fewer things happen over longer periods, and the exploration space is large. The reward is zero at every step except the last, where evading a missile pays the **miss distance** and being hit pays zero.\n",
    "\n",
    "Several of their design choices are the same ones we have already seen, arrived at independently. The action space is reduced to desired heading, altitude and throttle, with a lower-level autopilot handling the attitude control — the agent decides *where to go*, not how to move the control surfaces, which shrinks the search space and speeds convergence. In the one-versus-one scenario they go further, fixing the throttle at maximum, automating the missile launch, and removing variation in starting positions, precisely to keep the computation within one day on a single workstation.\n",
    "\n",
    "The results are also instructive. In the evasion scenarios the agent converges on the tactic human pilots are taught: **descend into denser air so that the missile decelerates** and turn away from the launch point. In the one-versus-one scenario it is consistently shot down at first, and does not fire at all — because it has not learned to approach closely enough to be in range — before gradually learning to launch its first missile and, later in training, its second. The authors also report that as the agent became better at dodging the adversary's first missile, the scripted adversary began using its second missile more often. Even against a *fixed* opponent, the distribution of situations the agent faces shifts as the agent improves.\n",
    "\n",
    "The cost of a scripted adversary is the obvious one: the learned policy is a counter to that script. It is a hypothesis about what would work against an opponent who behaves in a particular way, which is a useful thing to have, and not the same thing as a good policy."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Evaluation, verification and validation\n",
    "\n",
    "In the earlier chapters, evaluating a policy meant running it for a few hundred episodes and plotting the mean return. That works because the evaluation environment is the deployment environment. Here it is not, and the question \"is this policy good?\" has no comparably cheap answer.\n",
    "\n",
    "Three difficulties compound.\n",
    "\n",
    "**The state space cannot be covered.** Even for board games the state space is far too large to enumerate, as the games table showed. For a multi-domain operational problem, a brute-force statistical characterisation of failure is intractable. We therefore cannot produce the kind of failure-rate statistics that certification of conventional systems relies on.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Failure statistics assume a fixed input distribution.** Running a model many times to estimate how often it fails presupposes that the inputs are representative. If the environment shifts — which in this domain is the normal case, not the exception — the estimate is no longer about the situation the system is in.\n",
    "\n",
    "**The evidence is generated by the thing being tested.** Testing an RL policy means running it in a simulator, which is the same artefact it was trained in, with the same omissions. A policy that exploits a modelling artefact will pass a test built on that artefact.\n",
    "\n",
    "The RAND report's practical recommendation follows from the third point, and it is a good one: **test and evaluation should not be treated as separate from training.** Failure cases found during testing are training data. The report also suggests generative techniques for producing more realistic test cases, and adversarial search for finding failures that nobody anticipated — but it records honestly that the resulting process is a laborious loop of finding failure modes and retraining to remove them, with no termination criterion.\n",
    "\n",
    "Certification has an additional, uncomfortable property here. A conventional system is certified once against a specification. An RL policy's validity is tied to the distribution it was trained on, so a change in the world can invalidate it without anything in the software changing. The workshop participants' example is exact: if an RL system is used for route planning and a new surface-to-air missile with greater range and speed is fielded, the system could be **declared no longer certified for use**. That is a maintenance obligation of a kind that conventional software does not have."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## The risks that are not algorithmic\n",
    "\n",
    "It would be comfortable to end the chapter here, with a list of technical problems and the corresponding research directions. The most useful contribution of the [RAND risk assessment](./papers/(2024)%20-%20Risk%20Assessment%20of%20Reinforcement%20Learning%20AI%20Systems.pdf) is that it refuses to. Its subtitle — *Looking Beyond the Technology* — is the argument: a system can fail without the algorithm failing at all.\n",
    "\n",
    "The study's method matters for how much weight to put on what follows. The findings below come from a structured elicitation workshop with eleven participants from the U.S. Air Force, Army and RAND, who were asked to identify challenges, implications and mitigations for a hypothetical deployment of RL in an air operations centre, and then to rank them. These are therefore **expert judgements about a hypothetical scenario**, not measurements. They are valuable as a map of where the concerns lie, and should not be read as anything more."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Narrow versus broad, and fast versus incremental\n",
    "\n",
    "The workshop was organised around two axes that turn out to be the right ones.\n",
    "\n",
    "The first is **scope**. A *narrow* application does one bounded thing — replan a reconnaissance route, allocate a specific resource — and produces intermediate outputs that a trained human can evaluate. A *broad* application ingests everything and emits a decision. The report's finding is that the challenges do not grow smoothly with scope; several of them change character. Training data requirements grow, simulation models grow, test and evaluation becomes intractable, and — the point that recurs throughout — humans lose the ability to judge whether the output is sensible, because the process being replaced is larger than a person can hold in mind.\n",
    "\n",
    "The second is **pace**. The study contrasted a *crawl-walk-run* deployment, in which narrow systems are fielded first and scope grows as confidence is earned, with a *great leap*, in which a single large system is fielded in one step. The workshop ranked these very differently. The highest-priority challenge identified for the great leap was, in the participants' own words, that there was **no failure mode that is not catastrophic mission failure**, and it was judged both highly consequential and difficult to mitigate. The incremental path attracted concerns that were judged more likely but far less severe.\n",
    "\n",
    "For an engineer, the practical reading is that **the deployment strategy is part of the system design**, not a management decision taken afterwards. A narrow system whose output a human can check has a fallback. A broad one may not."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Trust, in both directions\n",
    "\n",
    "The black-box character of a deep policy creates a problem that is not solved by making the policy more accurate.\n",
    "\n",
    "The RAND report's illustrative case is from medicine rather than defence. Watson for Oncology was a machine-learning decision tool whose recommendations physicians found redundant when they agreed with their own diagnosis and suspicious when they did not; the deployment was ultimately judged a failure even though a later meta-analysis supported its value as a supplement to physician diagnosis. Distrust alone was enough to prevent use.\n",
    "\n",
    "The opposite failure is equally documented and, in this domain, more dangerous. The workshop identified **lack of trust** and **blind trust** as the second- and third-ranked challenges for the great-leap scenario, and both were considered problematic. The general phenomenon has a long history in the human-factors literature: [Parasuraman and Riley](https://doi.org/10.1518/001872097778543886) distinguish *misuse* of automation (over-reliance) from *disuse* (rejecting it), and show that both arise from a mismatch between the operator's trust and the system's actual reliability. Confirmation bias makes this worse in a specific way the RAND report names: an operator who sees the system agree with them on a case they understand may generalise that agreement into confidence about cases they do not.\n",
    "\n",
    "The architectural vocabulary for the response is familiar. **Human-in-the-loop** means the system proposes and a human authorises each action. **Human-on-the-loop** means the system acts and a human supervises, with authority to intervene. The distinction is only meaningful if the supervisor can actually evaluate what they are supervising, in the time available — which is exactly what a broad application removes, and exactly why the third-highest-ranked force-structure challenge in the incremental scenario was that **operators lack training for supervisory control** of the agents they are meant to oversee."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Dependence, atrophy and the cost of falling back\n",
    "\n",
    "The remaining risks are organisational, and they are the ones an algorithms course is least likely to mention.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Skill atrophy.** If an RL system performs the planning, planners stop planning. The workshop ranked atrophy of battle-planning workforce skill among its top concerns, with the observation that if the system fails or must be taken offline, the workforce may no longer be able to plan and oversee operations effectively. The fallback exists on paper and not in practice.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Detecting that something is wrong.** Identifying the need for retraining was ranked a top-three technical challenge in both employment concepts studied. The reason is worth understanding, because it is not a matter of monitoring effort. A catastrophic failure announces itself. A **gradual degradation** does not, and the workshop's phrase for it — \"boiling a frog\" — is apt. Combined with feedback that arrives 24 to 72 hours after the decision, the report records that a subtle problem could take five or ten days to notice.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Retraining is not free and not safe.** Once a failure is identified, someone must decide whether to take the system offline. If the failure is consequential there may be no choice. But the failure case was, by definition, not anticipated, so training examples for it may not exist and may be slow to generate; and examples drawn from a different distribution than the original training data can corrupt the existing policy. A full recertification before returning the system to service may be infeasible. The report's conclusion is that unless mitigation is planned in advance, a failure mode can become a critical problem simply because there is no safe route back.\n",
    "\n",
    "**People.** The single highest-ranked challenge in the entire study, across every category, was the absence of a workforce able to deploy and sustain these systems, followed immediately by the difficulty of recruiting and retaining such a workforce against much higher pay elsewhere. This is not a remark about defence organisations specifically. It is a general property of systems that require scarce expertise to remain correct: the system's reliability is bounded by the availability of the people who understand it.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Accountability.** The workshop discussion raised a question with no technical answer: if the system errs, who is responsible? The commander who acted on its recommendation, without reliable data? Whoever authorised the use of the black box? The report notes that accountability would likely fall on a human in the chain of command, without being able to say which one.\n",
    "\n",
    "The through-line is that **technical limitations and organisational limitations multiply rather than add**. An opaque policy is manageable if skilled operators can evaluate it; skilled operators are scarce; scarcity is worsened by the same automation that made them seem unnecessary; and the erosion is invisible until the system needs to be taken offline, which is the moment those operators are required."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<!-- FIGURE: a loop diagram of compounding organisational risk. Nodes: \"RL system fielded\" -> \"staffing reduced\" -> \"planning skills atrophy\" -> \"degradation harder to detect\" -> \"failure discovered late\" -> \"system taken offline\" -> \"no fallback capability\" -> back to \"pressure to keep the system running\". Caption: risks that are not algorithmic. -->"
   ]
