  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Concluding remarks\n",
    "\n",
    "The argument of this chapter can be compressed into a single asymmetry. **In a game, the simulation is the world. In a military application, the simulation is a model of the world, and the difference between the two is where the risk lives.** Everything else follows from that sentence: the fidelity-versus-speed trade-off, the sim-to-real gap, the impossibility of exhaustive test and evaluation, the dependence on pre-training and shaped rewards, the caution about deploying broad systems.\n",
    "\n",
    "That is not a conclusion that RL has no place here. The evidence in the supplied papers is, if anything, encouraging at the tactical end. Guidance, flight control and short-horizon air-combat problems have low-dimensional states, physics-based transitions, measurable rewards and fast simulators, and RL demonstrably produces working policies for them. The difficulty is not RL; it is *scope*. The RAND study's own summary is that the value lies in finding the \"sweet spot\" — missions narrow enough that the agent can be trained sufficiently, and whose failures can be mitigated enough to keep risk acceptable.\n",
    "\n",
    "Three habits of mind are worth taking from this chapter into any application of RL outside a game, military or otherwise.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Ask what the simulator omits before asking which algorithm to use.** The algorithm is the easy part, and it is nearly always the part that gets the attention. The environment determines what can be learned.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Read every reward function as an optimisation target, not as a description of intent.** The agent will find the maximum, including the maxima you did not anticipate. Our seven-by-seven grid found one in a reward function with four terms.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Treat the surrounding system as part of the system.** Whether a policy can be supervised, retrained, certified, and switched off are engineering requirements in exactly the same sense as sample efficiency, and they are usually the ones that decide whether the thing gets used."
   ]
  }
